<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gabès</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gabès is a coastal city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, on the gulf of the same name, built around a large seaside oasis. On the site of the Roman Tacapae, it was an important stop on the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> and Tripolitania. Around 1097 it became the seat of the Banu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a>, an Arab dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> conquered it in 1160. It is now a centre of the chemical and phosphate industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gabes.jpg" alt="Gabès" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ala Hachaichi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A7%D8%A1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B5%D9%8A%D8%AF_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%AD%D8%B1%D9%8A_-_%D9%82%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
